# ============================================================================
# Orchestrator — what NEVER goes remote.
#
# The tree is ~88% local state by size (code+docs 4.6 MB, state 29.8 MB), so
# this file is the boundary that makes a repo viable at all. Owner's hard rules:
# no evaluation databases stored remotely, no reliance on remote access to work.
#
# Runtime state proper lives OUTSIDE this tree at $ORCH_STATE_DIR (default
# ~/.codex/orchestrator, ~20 GB: the Brain, cadence stamps, worktrees, venvs,
# the capability ledger). That separation is why a second instance needs only a
# different ORCH_STATE_DIR, not a code change.
#
# NOTE ON FORMAT: git does NOT support trailing comments on pattern lines. The
# first version of this file put explanatory comments after each pattern, which
# silently made every pattern inert — 795 files staged instead of 141, including
# the Brain export. Keep comments on their own lines, and verify with
# `git check-ignore -v` or `git status`, never with a hand-rolled parser.
# ============================================================================

# --- evaluation & experiment output: machine-local by the owner's explicit rule ---
# experiments/ is 17 MB / 390 files of per-arm logs, diffs and ship gates.
experiments/
# ux_reviews/ is 2.6 MB / 262 files of UX panel transcripts and scores.
ux_reviews/
# data/ holds feedback-snapshot.json (1.9 MB) — a Brain export, not source.
data/
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.db
# GitHub rate-limit accounting for THIS machine only.
gh-rate-ledger.ndjson

# --- per-RUN execution telemetry the CI runner emits INTO the checkout ---
# stranske/Workflows `reusable-codex-run.yml` writes langsmith-fleet-worker-attempt.json
# to the checkout ROOT on every agent round (step "Write worker model attempt artifact"),
# purely to stage the actions/upload-artifact step on the next line. That upload is the
# real transport, so keeping the file out of git loses nothing, and nothing in this tree
# reads the path -- the emitters here are langsmith_*.py (underscores) and they read
# langsmith-fleet.ndjson out of $ORCH_STATE_DIR.
#
# It was TRACKED from 2118f57 until 2026-08-23, holding whichever run merged last: a
# tool-vs-evidence violation (CLAUDE.md 1), and a second unmanaged store of the
# `operation_role: worker` provenance that is supposed to reach the learner through
# feedback.py's tables (CLAUDE.md 2). Ten commits across PRs #59/#61/#62/#63 rewrote it,
# producing one add/add conflict per concurrent PR and silently widening diffs that had
# nothing to do with their change -- that is what broke PR #66 (pr_number 66 against
# main's 61) and what turned this branch's own rebase into a modify/delete conflict when
# #68 merged underneath it.
#
# Untracking loses no provenance: the producer already uploads the same bytes as Actions
# artifact `langsmith-fleet-v1-worker-attempt-<pr>` with 30-day retention, and that upload
# is the only reason the file is written at all.
#
# UNTRACKING is what makes these patterns bite, and the order is not interchangeable:
# `git add -A` skips an ignored UNTRACKED path but stages an ignored TRACKED one, so
# ignoring alone would have changed nothing while the path stayed in the index.
#
# A pattern rather than the literal name: the runner names the file after the role it
# recorded, so a verifier or evaluator attempt would arrive as a sibling and escape a
# literal. Bounded to the two extensions the langsmith-fleet/v1 schema actually emits.
#
# ROOT-ANCHORED, and the leading slash is load-bearing. Unanchored, a gitignore pattern
# matches at EVERY depth, so `langsmith-fleet*.json` would also swallow a contract schema
# at docs/contracts/schemas/langsmith-fleet-v1.schema.json -- silently making a tracked,
# load-bearing file untrackable. That is not hypothetical: stranske/Workflows tracks
# exactly that path, and this tree already keeps its sibling
# docs/contracts/schemas/artifact-manifest-v1.schema.json. It is the same near-miss the
# vendored-node_modules exception records, and the same conclusion -- the anchored pattern
# is the safe one. Only the runner's ROOT-level write is debris.
#
# The nested CI artifact directory is named separately for the same reason: anchoring it
# to the one directory that holds it cannot reach a schema somewhere else.
#
# Deliberately ABOVE the template-managed block below: a Template-Version bump rewrites
# that block, which would silently drop this. The fleet-wide home is the same pattern in
# stranske/Workflows templates/consumer-repo/.gitignore.
/langsmith-fleet*.json
/langsmith-fleet*.ndjson
artifacts/langsmith/

# --- durable audit history belongs to the workspace, not the tool ---
# Code/Audits/Orchestrator/ is the system-of-record for dated decision records;
# capability_admission.commitments() reads it from there. A second copy here
# would be a parallel inventory — the failure mode this project is named for.
Audits/

# --- python / tooling noise ---
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# --- lock and backup artifacts ---
*.lock
*.bak
*.pre-ingest-fix-*

# --- editor / OS ---
.DS_Store
*.swp

# --- machine-local policy and evidence (the tool is public; the evidence is not) ---
# The split is tool-vs-evidence: generic capabilities and gates ship, while this
# instance's failure history, spend calibration and availability stay local.
# Sanitized public twins of the load-bearing docs are committed under their
# canonical names; these are the full versions.
#
# IMPROVEMENT_BACKLOG.md IS NO LONGER LISTED, ON PURPOSE. Ignoring it made it
# invisible: a gitignored file does not exist in a git worktree, and agents work
# in worktrees, so CLAUDE.md's two mandatory rules about it were unfollowable by
# the workers they bind. The log now lives OUTSIDE the tree at
# $ORCH_LOCAL_RUNTIME, reached via improvement_log.py, and the tracked
# IMPROVEMENT_BACKLOG.md here is a short POINTER to it. Keeping it ignored as
# well would let the 481 KB evidence file reappear at that path silently, which
# is the failure this whole change undoes; tracked, it announces itself in
# `git status` instead. test_improvement_log.py fails if the pointer grows into
# a log.
LOCAL_POLICY.md
CAPABILITY_USEFULNESS.md
*.local.md

